 ],
 "literal": "Tethering post disliking, sighing elephants with, stable disliking, / wind-natured horses neighing, in that place, / sand-filled courtyard entered noble men, / even if they do not eat, with himself swearing, / \"Eat!\" saying, entreating, great-famed Sāttaṉ, / here he is of sweet gentle nature; to enemies, / where striking weapons mingle in terrifying battle, / those with toddy-filled vessels, who spoke inside the town / boastful words, having forgotten them, those petty men of small fame, / when they retreat in fear, / as a shield, he himself steps forward!",
 "translation": "In his sand-filled courtyard where elephants, weary of their tethering posts, stand sighing, and wind-swift horses neigh, weary of their stables, noble men enter. Even if they do not wish to eat, the great-famed Sāttaṉ swears an oath upon himself and begs them, 'Please eat!' Here, he is of such sweet and gentle nature. Yet, in the terrifying battle where striking weapons clash in confusion, when petty men—who once boasted loudly in the town over their cups of toddy—forget their brave words and retreat in fear, Sāttaṉ steps forward to the very front, standing as a protective shield for all.",
 "modern": "கட்டப்பட்ட கம்பை வெறுத்து மூச்சுவிடும் யானைகளுடனும், லாயத்தை வெறுத்து கனைக்கும் காற்று போன்ற வேகமுடைய குதிரைகளுடனும் விளங்கும் அந்த இடத்தில், மணல் நிறைந்த முற்றத்தில் நுழைந்த சான்றோர்கள் உண்ணாதிருந்தாலும், தன் மேல் ஆணையிட்டு \"உண்ணுங்கள்\" என்று இரந்து கேட்கும் பெரும் புகழுடைய சாத்தன், இங்கு இனிய மென்மையான இயல்புடையவனாக இருக்கிறான். ஆனால், பகைவர்கள் வீசும் படைகள் கலந்து போரிடும் அச்சம் தரும் போர்க்களத்தில், கள்ளுண்ட கலங்களை ஏந்தி ஊருக்குள் வீரமாகப் பேசிய நெடுமொழிகளை மறந்து, அஞ்சி ஓடும் சிறிய மனிதர்கள் விலகிச் செல்லும்போது, அவர்களுக்குப் பாதுகாப்பாகத் தானே முன்வந்து முன்னின்று காப்பான்.",
 "explanation": "The poet Āvūr Mūlaṅkiḻār praises the chieftain Pāṇṭiyaṉ Kīrañcāttaṉ (also known as Sāttaṉ). The poem highlights the contrast between the chieftain's extreme gentleness and hospitality at home and his fierce, protective heroism on the battlefield. In his courtyard, Sāttaṉ is a warm host who practically begs his guests to eat, swearing oaths on himself to persuade them. However, on the battlefield, when lesser men who boasted of their bravery while drinking toddy in the safety of the village flee in terror, Sāttaṉ stands firm at the front as a shield to protect his retreating men. The restless elephants and horses in the courtyard symbolize his constant readiness for war, yet his domestic life is filled with gentle hospitality. The contrast between the boastful but cowardly men (ciṟu pērāḷar) and the silent, action-oriented hero (perumpeyar cāttaṉ) emphasizes his true nobility. Key words include 'irakkum' (entreating/begging) showing his humility at home, and 'ēmam' (shield/protection) showing his protective valor in battle.",
